WEBVTT

NOTE
Canonical written narration timed from the exact local narration render receipt.
Pronunciation-only substitutions are intentionally absent from caption text.

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.314
This chapter asks a specific question: The stack has owners for

2
00:00:05.314 --> 00:00:09.136
requests, constitutions, value conflict, optimization, planning, and self-improvement,

3
00:00:09.136 --> 00:00:14.390
but those layers presuppose an operational objective they do not create.

4
00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:20.288
Without a positive objective-formation lifecycle, a temporary request, learned preference estimate,

5
00:00:20.288 --> 00:00:24.835
or convenient metric can silently become a durable goal.

6
00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:30.264
The tempting shortcut is insufficient: A reward function, preference model,

7
00:00:30.264 --> 00:00:35.862
constitutional rule set, or natural-language mission does not by itself preserve

8
00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:41.460
the distinction between authorized purpose, contested value, target property, measurable proxy,

9
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.005
training signal, evaluator, planning criterion,

10
00:00:44.345 --> 00:00:48.625
persistence, and reauthorization when people or ontologies change.

11
00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:54.474
The chapter's core claim is this: A durable objective should be

12
00:00:54.474 --> 00:00:59.723
usable only through a versioned target-property contract that binds authority and

13
00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:04.971
affected parties to target/proxy causal assumptions, uncertainty and dissent, consumer-specific use,

14
00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:06.880
tampering tests, generalization limits,

15
00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:13.354
ontology version, expiry, reauthorization, and retirement; proxy improvement, predicted preference, reward,

16
00:01:13.354 --> 00:01:19.489
evaluator approval, or formal record validity alone establishes neither the right

17
00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:23.950
objective, moral truth, stable alignment, nor safe optimization.

18
00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:30.549
Create an objective charter naming purpose, principal, affected parties, constitutional ceilings,

19
00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:35.670
unresolved conflicts, normative and preference evidence, and explicit non-goals.

20
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.832
A concrete implementation trace makes the proposal testable.

21
00:01:40.832 --> 00:01:46.534
Implement an objective-contract registry with target/proxy graphs, consumer bindings, expiry,

22
00:01:46.534 --> 00:01:47.675
and invalidation.

23
00:01:48.015 --> 00:01:52.967
Test it in small environments containing known latent targets,

24
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:59.020
deliberately misspecified proxies, preference uncertainty, tampering, distribution shift, and ontology changes.

25
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.514
Compare fixed reward, ordinary preference learning, contract-governed optimization,

26
00:02:03.514 --> 00:02:09.226
and an oracle-bound control without claiming that a toy environment discovers

27
00:02:09.226 --> 00:02:10.265
human values.

28
00:02:10.605 --> 00:02:15.327
Separate the target property from every proxy, reward, evaluator, benchmark,

29
00:02:15.327 --> 00:02:19.105
planner heuristic, and deployment decision that consumes it;

30
00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:22.410
record the causal assumptions for each binding.

31
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.631
Represent uncertainty, dissent, aggregation rules, domain and temporal limits,

32
00:02:27.631 --> 00:02:33.055
and the conditions under which clarification or abstention outranks optimization.

33
00:02:33.655 --> 00:02:39.157
The design can still fail. reward misspecification Goodhart effects goal misgeneralization

34
00:02:39.157 --> 00:02:44.658
preference manipulation The correct response is to stop, narrow, quarantine, compensate,

35
00:02:44.658 --> 00:02:49.660
or retain an owned residual rather than narrate uncertainty away.

36
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:55.306
This chapter remains Design rationale at argument support. Its contracts, examples,

37
00:02:55.306 --> 00:02:59.435
tests, or local artifacts establish only their encoded scope.

38
00:02:59.435 --> 00:03:04.481
The chapter names proof targets rather than pretending they are already

39
00:03:04.481 --> 00:03:04.940
closed.

40
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.458
A finite Governed Objective Formation, Value Learning, and Goal Integrity record

41
00:03:10.458 --> 00:03:15.165
may hand off only when identity, authority, version, required checks,

42
00:03:15.165 --> 00:03:20.344
and residual ownership are present; no theorem grants empirical effectiveness or

43
00:03:20.344 --> 00:03:21.285
release authority.

44
00:03:21.625 --> 00:03:26.191
Proxy improvement never implies target-property improvement without separate evidence.

45
00:03:26.191 --> 00:03:31.773
Predicted preference is evidence about a person, not authority over that

46
00:03:31.773 --> 00:03:32.280
person.

47
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.844
No chapter claim or evidence state is promoted by this visual.

48
00:03:37.844 --> 00:03:42.808
A named mechanism is not proof of correct implementation or useful

49
00:03:42.808 --> 00:03:47.772
deployment. A local check or formal model does not establish real-world

50
00:03:47.772 --> 00:03:52.285
enforcement. No safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI, or ASI conclusion follows.

51
00:03:52.625 --> 00:03:57.100
These boundaries matter because an explanatory derivative is useful only if

52
00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.355
it preserves the evidence ceiling of its source.

53
00:04:00.955 --> 00:04:06.651
At most, this visual explains the chapter's design rationale at argument

54
00:04:06.651 --> 00:04:11.829
support; it adds no empirical, deployment, safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI,

55
00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:17.007
or ASI result. The next chapter is Institutions, International Coordination,

56
00:04:17.007 --> 00:04:18.560
and Public Legitimacy.

57
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.240
It takes responsibility for ASI-scale systems cross organizations, jurisdictions, borders,

58
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:30.113
public services, and affected populations. Technical permissions and private governance cannot

59
00:04:30.113 --> 00:04:34.385
determine who has public authority, whose participation counts,

60
00:04:34.385 --> 00:04:37.055
how conflicting rules are resolved.
